    paragraphs: [
      `Japanese keyword research is harder than English keyword research for one simple reason: one concept can take several written forms, and search demand and results differ from form to form. Japanese uses kanji, hiragana and katakana together, borrows English words in katakana, and people also type romaji and plain English. Google treats many variants as equivalent, but not all of them, so we check the actual results page for each important variant instead of assuming.`,
      `Some examples show the problem. A dental clinic needs to know whether people near it type ${ja("歯医者")} (the everyday word for dentist) or ${ja("歯科")} (the formal word used in clinic names); in practice both matter, for different pages. A web company must cover ${ja("ホームページ制作")}, ${ja("HP制作")} and ${ja("Web制作")}. A moving company sees ${ja("引っ越し")} and ${ja("引越し")}, two spellings of the same word. A ramen shop may be found through ${ja("ラーメン")} in katakana or ${ja("らーめん")} in hiragana, and the SEO industry itself is searched as ${ja("SEO対策")}, literally SEO measures, more often than as SEO alone.`,
      `Area words matter as much as the service. Japanese local searches often combine a prefecture, city, ward or station with the service, such as ${ja("渋谷駅 歯医者")} (dentist near Shibuya Station) or ${ja("梅田 美容院")} (hair salon in Umeda). The station name is often more important than the ward, because people live and work around train lines. For real estate, recruitment and home services, the combination of station, line and service defines which pages a site needs.`,
