    paragraphs: [
      `Japanese SEO companies sell their work in a few standard ways, and the model matters as much as the price. Understanding them makes quotes easier to compare, including ours.`,
      `<strong>Monthly consulting retainers.</strong> The most common model: a fixed monthly fee, usually with a minimum contract period, for analysis, advice and reporting. The key question is who implements the advice. Many consulting contracts deliver recommendations while your web company or your staff make the changes, which means results depend on how quickly someone else acts. Ask for a sample report and a list of what the consultant will change directly.`,
      `<strong>Pay-for-performance (${ja("成果報酬型")}).</strong> Here you pay only when chosen keywords rank, typically a daily or monthly fee for each keyword while it sits inside an agreed ranking band. It sounds low-risk, but it creates the wrong incentives. The vendor has every reason to choose keywords that are easy to rank and rarely searched; a top-ten ranking for a phrase nobody types brings no customers. Fees can rise just as rankings you would have earned anyway arrive, and some vendors use purchased links to reach targets quickly, leaving the risk of a manual action on your domain after the contract ends. If you consider this model, insist on choosing the keywords yourself and ask in writing what link building methods are used.`,
      `<strong>Per-article content fees.</strong> Content agencies charge per article, commonly ¥30,000 to ¥100,000 depending on length, research and whether a qualified professional supervises it. Check whether keyword research is included, how many revisions you get, who owns the text, and whether each article fits a plan or is simply volume. Twenty unconnected articles rarely do as much as five that answer the questions your buyers actually ask.`,
      `<strong>Fixed-price technical audits.</strong> A one-off audit, commonly around ¥200,000 to ¥300,000, gives you a list of issues. It is useful if you have developers ready to implement it, and much less useful as a document that sits in a shared folder.`,
      `<strong>Our model.</strong> We charge a flat monthly fee from ${both("Monthly SEO")} for a written scope that includes implementation. There are no ranking-based fees, no link packages and no per-keyword pricing. We report on impressions, clicks and enquiries alongside rankings for a sample of terms, because rankings alone are a poor measure of what SEO is worth to a business.`,
