  {
    id: "technical-seo",
    heading: "Technical SEO for Japanese websites",
    paragraphs: [
      `<strong>Character encoding.</strong> Every page, feed, sitemap and form should use UTF-8. Older Japanese sites and back-office systems sometimes still use Shift_JIS or EUC-JP, which causes garbled text (${ja("文字化け")}) when pages are shared, when forms send email, or when product feeds and CSV exports move between systems. We find mixed encodings during the audit and convert them in one controlled step.`,
      `<strong>URLs.</strong> A URL may contain Japanese, but paste it into an email or chat and every character is escaped: one kanji becomes a nine-character code such as %E6%9D%B1, and a four-character slug turns into three dozen symbols. Search engines read either form, and neither helps or hurts rankings by itself. Our default is a short slug in English or romaji, with the Japanese keyword carried by the title and the headings, because those links survive being shared, printed and typed. Whatever format you choose, the rule that outweighs the choice is to redirect every old address before a format ever changes.`,
      `<strong>Language markup.</strong> The html element should declare ${ja("lang=\"ja\"")} on Japanese pages, and English phrases or product names inside Japanese text can carry their own lang attribute. That helps screen readers pronounce text correctly and removes ambiguity for search engines. Bilingual sites need hreflang between language versions, covered in the international section.`,
      `<strong>JavaScript rendering.</strong> Sites built as single-page applications often send an almost empty page and build the content in the browser. Google can render JavaScript, but it takes extra time and resources, and many other crawlers, including a number of AI crawlers, do not run it at all. We prefer server-side rendering or static generation for any page that should rank, and we check what the raw HTML contains, not just what the browser shows.`,
      `<strong>Core Web Vitals with Japanese fonts.</strong> A complete Japanese typeface is a heavy download, because it has to cover kana plus thousands of kanji, and loading one on every page delays Largest Contentful Paint and can shift the layout when it finally arrives. Our fixes are to rely on the Japanese fonts already installed on phones and computers, or to cut the web font down to only the characters the site uses, to control font-display, and to reserve the space images and banners will occupy. Third-party tags are the other common problem on Japanese sites: chat widgets, heatmaps, retargeting tags and campaign scripts pile up over the years and slow down Interaction to Next Paint. We list every tag, its owner and its purpose, and remove the ones nobody can explain.`,
      `<strong>Duplicate URLs and crawl waste.</strong> Common causes are www and non-www versions, http and https, index.html paths, trailing slash variants, tracking parameters and, on property and ecommerce sites, filter combinations that create near-infinite URLs. Some older Japanese sites also run a separate smartphone site under a path such as /sp/. We consolidate with redirects and canonicals, block crawl traps, and keep sitemaps limited to pages you actually want indexed.`,
      `<strong>Structured data.</strong> We add Organization, LocalBusiness, BreadcrumbList, Product, Article and Person markup where it is accurate, with Japanese names and addresses written consistently. Google now shows FAQ rich results for very few sites, so we use FAQ markup for clarity rather than expecting extra space in the results.`,
