  {
    id: "content-eeat",
    heading: "Japanese content that ranks: E-E-A-T, supervision and native writers",
    paragraphs: [
      `Google's quality guidelines describe experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust, usually shortened to E-E-A-T, and they matter most for topics that affect health, money, safety or legal rights. In those fields, pages backed by a named, qualified person tend to do better in Japanese results, and Japanese publishers have a well-established convention for showing who that person is.`,
      `<strong>Supervision (${ja("監修")}).</strong> The convention is to name a qualified professional as the content's supervisor: a doctor (${ja("医師")}) for medical pages, a lawyer (${ja("弁護士")}) for legal topics, a tax accountant (${ja("税理士")}) for tax questions, a licensed real estate transaction agent (${ja("宅地建物取引士")}) for property, or a registered dietitian for food and nutrition. The supervisor's name, qualification and a short profile appear on the page, and the profile has its own page. We build these supervisor and author pages into the site structure, link them from every article they cover and mark them up with Person structured data. What we never do is invent a supervisor or attach a name to content the person has not actually reviewed.`,
      `<strong>How the writing works.</strong> For each page we write an English brief with the Japanese target keywords, the questions to answer, a heading outline, facts that need a source, internal links and the supervisor needed. Your writer drafts in Japanese. We then check that the headings follow the plan, the title and description fit in search results, internal links and structured data are in place, and the page loads quickly, before publishing. If the writer's draft departs from the plan for a good reason, such as a better way of explaining something to Japanese readers, we adjust the plan, not the writer.`,
      `<strong>Formats that work for Japanese readers.</strong> Japanese buyers read carefully before contacting a business, and pages that answer everything up front convert better. Price tables with tax-inclusive figures, a step-by-step explanation of how the service works (${ja("流れ")}), a frequently asked questions section (${ja("よくある質問")}), clear information about who is responsible, and real examples of your own work all help. We avoid invented testimonials and exaggerated claims, which are both risky under Japanese advertising rules and easy for readers to see through.`,
      `<strong>AI-written content.</strong> AI tools can help a writer research or outline, but Google's spam policies target content produced at scale mainly to manipulate rankings, however it is made. We do not publish unreviewed AI text in Japanese or any other language. Older sites often have the opposite problem: many similar articles written over the years that compete for the same keyword. Merging them into one strong page and redirecting the rest is one of the quickest wins in Japanese SEO.`,
