  {
    id: "meo-local-seo",
    heading: "MEO in Japan: Google Business Profile, reviews and portal sites",
    paragraphs: [
      `In Japan, local search optimisation is usually called MEO (${ja("MEO対策")}), map engine optimisation, because so many local searches end in the map results. For clinics, restaurants, salons, schools, trades and shops, the map pack is often worth more than the first organic result.`,
      `<strong>The profile belongs to you.</strong> Your Google Business Profile must be created and verified by your company at the real address where you serve customers, or as a service-area business if you visit customers instead. Google chooses the verification method, which can include a video or a postcard, and your staff complete it. We give you step-by-step instructions in English with screenshots your team can follow, and afterwards you add us as a manager. We never create profiles in our own account, never use virtual office addresses, and never add keywords to your business name, which breaks Google's guidelines and can get a listing suspended.`,
      `<strong>What we optimise.</strong> The primary and secondary categories, services and products, opening hours including special hours for Golden Week, Obon and the New Year holidays, attributes, photos of the entrance, interior and staff, regular posts, and the link to the right page on your site rather than always the home page. The location page on your website repeats the same name, address and phone number, gives directions from the nearest station and exit, explains parking, and carries LocalBusiness structured data.`,
      `<strong>Addresses written consistently.</strong> Japanese addresses can be written many ways: chome, banchi and go in kanji or with hyphens, full-width or half-width numbers, with or without the building name and floor. Search engines match inconsistent versions reasonably well, but customers and portals copy whatever they find. We pick one canonical form in Japanese and one in English and use them everywhere.`,
      `<strong>Reviews.</strong> Reviews are one of the strongest local signals and one of the most sensitive areas. Ask every customer, not only happy ones, reply politely in Japanese to each review including critical ones, and never offer rewards or discounts in exchange for reviews, which Google's policies prohibit. Japan's stealth marketing rules, in force since October 2023, treat advertising disguised as independent opinion as a misleading representation, so paid or staff-written reviews that pose as genuine customer opinions are a legal risk as well as a breach of Google's rules.`,
      `<strong>Working with portals.</strong> Tabelog, Hot Pepper Beauty, SUUMO, Jalan and similar portals rank for many local searches and bring bookings, but usually charge fees or commissions. We treat them as partners: keep your listings accurate, link them to your own site where the portal allows it, and give customers who find you on a portal a reason to book directly next time, such as a LINE Official Account, a members' page or a direct-booking benefit.`,
